import * as React from "react";
import type { HeadFC, PageProps } from "gatsby";
import Layout from "../common/layout";
import { Body } from "../common/commonStyles";

import styled from "@emotion/styled";

const Main = styled.div`
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  line-height: 1.4;
  text-size: 1.5rem;
  padding: 2rem 4rem 2rem 4rem;
`;

const Title = styled.h1`
  text-size: 3rem;
  text-align: center;
`;

const SubTitle = styled.h2`
  text-size: 2rem;
  text-align: right;
  padding-right: 5rem;
`;

const Separator = styled.div`
  color: #99a893;
  text-size: 20rem;
  text-align: center;
  height: 50px;
  padding-bottom: 1rem;
`;

const MutedPage: React.FC<PageProps> = () => {
  return (
    <Layout
      page="Short Stories"
      title="Merlin May - Aphasia"
      SeoDescription="Following the events of John's story. Jayce has always been the communicator, being able to achieve anything he wanted through the power of his words. Aphasia tells the story of Jayce and his struggle to communicate when his speech is taken away by a sudden stroke."
      SeoKeywords={[
        "Merlin May",
        "Author",
        "Author Merlin May",
        "fiction writer Merlin May",
      ]}
    >
      <Body>
        <Main>
          <Title>Aphasia</Title>
          <SubTitle>by Merlin May</SubTitle>

          <p>
            Jayce arrived at John&apos;s place. If he were to turn off his brain
            and head home, he knew he would end up at John&apos;s place, not at
            his. The route to his flat was programmed into his spinal cord by
            now. Jayce had had a day. He&apos;d been struggling with the onset
            of a mighty headache from the beginning of the last meeting. He
            needed to be home, and home was John. Usually, venting his problems
            as John was preparing dinner would release the tension of a long,
            exhausting day. It was almost a ritual between the two of them. Once
            upon a time, Jayce had tried to help cook, but after a few attempts
            that were an affront to the senses, he was permanently delegated to
            clean-up duty.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            The throbbing pain he&apos;d felt on the way to John&apos;s turned
            sharper with every passing moment, but Jayce still hoped his usual
            prescription of a good rant followed by a hearty meal would soon
            resolve his ache. Relief refused to come. The pain only increased,
            making it hard to focus on what he was saying. His vision got blurry
            as the room started to spin around him. He raised his hand to rub
            his nose bridge. The movement was awkward, as if his hand was not
            his own. At that moment, a sharp sense of panic settled in his gut.
            He tried to stand up, but some of his muscles failed to respond, and
            he crashed over. His mouth opened to call for help, but the sound
            was refusing to come out. Panic spread across his body, rivalling
            the pain of the headache. His vision failing, his body not
            responding, panic turned to doom, and a few solitary tears streamed
            down his face.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            A combination of Jayce&apos;s unusual silence and the unexpected
            thud gave John pause. He went to check on Jayce, only to find him
            toppled over. He rushed to his side. Jayce felt John&apos;s gentle
            touch, but he could not respond. He only saw his blurred shadow in
            the light of the room, moving. John&apos;s hands started patting
            Jayce&apos;s trouser pockets.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;<em>Buy a guy dinner first</em>,&quot; Jayce thought,
            surprising himself that even in what could very well be the very
            last moments of his life, his mind still decided to make an
            inappropriate joke out of it.
          </p>
          <p>
            John pulled Jayce&apos;s phone from his pocket. Jayce wondered what
            he was going to do with that. Soon after, an unfamiliar voice hit
            his ears, but the words failed to register. Was that... John&apos;s
            voice? He&apos;d thought John to be mute, and the surprise made him
            temporarily forget his own predicament. A woman&apos;s voice
            responded soon after, and Jayce felt John move his body. John rolled
            Jayce onto his side, gently supporting his neck. He was probably
            following the instructions of the woman on the phone. Then they both
            waited. The unfamiliar sound of John&apos;s voice filled the time
            like a metronome. It was a smooth bass voice repeating the same
            indistinguishable words, like a lullaby. There was a slight tremor
            in John&apos;s hand as he tried to pat Jayce&apos;s back
            reassuringly. He continued to do so as the sound of the ambulance
            sirens could be heard in the distance. The emergency services took
            him away. John stayed by Jayce&apos;s side until they got to the
            hospital.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            Upon arriving at the hospital, Jayce was taken to the emergency room
            for testing. The lack of a wait in itself was telling how urgent the
            situation was. John waited and waited. For a while, there was no
            news.&nbsp;&nbsp;Then, a nurse approached John.
          </p>
          <p>&quot;Are you the individual who came with Mr. Young?&quot;</p>
          <p>John nodded.</p>
          <p>&quot;What is your relation with the patient?&quot;</p>
          <p>
            John hesitated; there were many words that could be used to describe
            his relationship with Jayce. Business partner, friend, colleague...
            yet none told the whole story. The nurse must have noticed his
            distress, for she decided to put John out of his misery and added:
          </p>
          <p>&quot;Are you a friend?&quot;</p>
          <p>John nodded again.&nbsp;</p>
          <p>
            &quot;Do you happen to know a Mr...&quot; the nurse paused,
            &quot;John Smith? We have him down as Mr Young&apos;s emergency
            contact and medical proxy. But we are unable to reach him.&quot;
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;That&apos;s me,&quot; John said so quietly the nurse almost
            didn&apos;t catch it. John was surprised to hear he was Jayce&apos;s
            emergency contact. True, his parents were in Singapore, and they
            rarely spoke. Still, why would Jayce put down his contact, of all
            people? He likely had a lot of other friends who were at least more
            reliable... well, not reliable, but at least more...
            talkative.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;Ah...&quot; the nurse said, like a mystery had been solved.
            The look she gave John changed to something softer. There was a look
            of expectation in her eyes, but John could not think of what it
            could mean. The nurse coughed and regained her colder composure.
            &quot;The patient has suffered a hemorrhagic stroke, he was lucky to
            make it to the hospital in time. We are not sure yet how impaired
            his mental faculties are; however, as far as we can tell, he cannot
            communicate with us. As such, as his guardian, you will have to sign
            off on his surgery.&quot;
          </p>
          <p>
            John nodded. The word stroke lingered in his mind. It was a big,
            scary word.&nbsp;<em>Stroke.</em>&nbsp;John&apos;s grandfather had
            had a stroke, he didn&apos;t survive much longer afterwards. The
            once stoic John Smith felt a chill in his chest, spreading through
            his body as if someone had poured liquid nitrogen on him.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>&quot;Did you understand?&quot; the nurse asked.&nbsp;</p>
          <p>
            John looked at the nurse wide-eyed. What was he supposed to
            understand?&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;It&apos;s ok, buddy, the doctor will explain everything again.
            Follow me, so you can see your,&quot; she paused, &quot;friend
            before they have to take him in for surgery.&quot;
          </p>
          <p>
            John followed quietly behind. The edges of his vision were blurry,
            and he stayed close to the nurse. It felt like the world was
            collapsing onto him, and he was following that one tiny
            will-o&apos;-the-wisp showing him the way through the dark. And show
            the way it did, all the way to an even louder room, with beds full
            of patients. Doctors and nurses alike were running all around the
            place constantly. The nurse pushed a curtain aside to show Jayce
            lying still in bed. At a nicer time, it would have been funny to
            observe how, despite the fact his eyes were open, Jayce was quiet.
            Which, for a man who was only silent when he was asleep, was an
            unsettling sight to behold. A woman wearing a hospital gown stood
            over Jayce, looking at some black and white pictures, which at a
            glance looked like scans. John assumed correctly that she was the
            doctor in charge.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;Are you the guardian?&quot; she asked, lifting her eyes from
            the tablet at the sound of the curtain opening.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;I will get the documentation ready,&quot; the nurse said and
            left.
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;I am Doctor Patel. I will be the surgeon on Mr. Young&rsquo;s
            case. There are multiple areas of bleeding in his brain, likely due
            to a previously undiagnosed vascular malformation. The largest
            haemorrhage is in the left frontal lobe, affecting his ability to
            speak. There is also a smaller bleed in his left temporal lobe,
            which may temporarily impact his ability to understand language. We
            need to operate immediately to stop the bleeding, drain excess
            blood, and relieve the pressure inside his skull before further
            damage occurs.&quot;
          </p>
          <p>&quot;Will he die?&quot;John asked.</p>
          <p>
            &quot;While these are serious injuries, we caught it early, so I am
            optimistic about his chances. However, because of the multiple
            haemorrhages, his recovery may take longer, and we won&rsquo;t fully
            know the extent of his cognitive and language impairment until the
            swelling subsides. If you would like a second opinion, we can
            provide one, but time is of the essence, and we strongly recommend
            proceeding with surgery now.&quot;
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;I will sign. Female doctors are statistically 30% more likely
            to have a positive outcome. Please do your best,&quot; John said
            matter-of-factly.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            Jayce looked at the scene unfolding around him. John&rsquo;s mouth
            moved, but the words dissolved before reaching Jayce. He understood
            pieces, like his name, something about surgery, but they drifted
            apart like scattered puzzle pieces of a jigsaw left at the mercy of
            a clowder of cats.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            Whatever he said seemed to catch the doctor off guard. Jayce tried
            to chuckle, but nothing came. His throat tightened; his lips
            didn&rsquo;t obey. He was trapped in his mind, and all he could do
            was watch.
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;I assume you&apos;re referring to the study on surgical
            outcomes? Regardless, I assure you we&rsquo;ll do everything we can
            for him,&quot; Dr. Patel&apos;s voice was steady and professional,
            careful not to overpromise. &quot;I&rsquo;ll give you two a moment
            before we take him to the operating room.&quot;
          </p>
          <p>
            John went beside Jayce&apos;s bed and took his hand gently into his.
            It fell limp, but it was warm. They stared into each other&rsquo;s
            eyes. John&apos;s expression, despite his best efforts, was not the
            most reassuring. He stared at Jayce&apos;s face as if it was the
            last time he would be seeing it, as if he was trying to remember
            every detail. Jayce had always been the happy one in their
            partnership. Now, John didn&apos;t know how to cheer him up. Then
            Jayce exhaled one deep breath and winked. A soft smile broke
            John&apos;s sober expression. Stroke or no stroke, Jayce was still
            Jayce.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            Whatever moment they were having was interrupted by the nurse
            returning with the release forms and the orderlies arriving to wheel
            Jayce away.
          </p>
          <Separator>{"\u273C"}</Separator>
          <p>
            John paced back and forth in the waiting room. His heart was beating
            loudly in his chest, and a feeling of restlessness had taken over
            his body. There was nothing he could do. He knew that. Logically. He
            was usually good at stepping back when he couldn&apos;t do anything.
            Why was it so hard now? The nurse from earlier patted him on the
            back, and he turned around with a jump.
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;If you keep walking like that, you&apos;ll dig a hole into the
            floor,&quot; she joked.
          </p>
          <p>
            John recoiled slightly and gave her a wide-eyed, apologetic look,
            telling her he didn&apos;t take the joke well. She retreated, giving
            him enough space to relax a little.
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;I meant it as a joke, sorry. Your... friend is in good
            hands,&quot; she said calmly. &quot;You know, I have a feeling you
            won&apos;t leave his side once he&apos;s out, so while you have the
            time, go and make sure you sort out the things you need to, like
            notifying family, workplaces... I can give you a list of things
            people usually need to sort out, I understand it&apos;s a bit of an
            overwhelming time to think...&quot;
          </p>
          <p>John nodded appreciatively.&nbsp;</p>
          <p>
            &quot;If you want, I can put a note on the nurse&apos;s desk to text
            you once he is out of surgery. He would be under general
            anaesthetic, so you&apos;ll have plenty of time to return, as he
            won&apos;t be awake.&quot;
          </p>
          <p>
            The nurse gave John a little printed list off a desk. He traced the
            bullet points with his fingers, it looked clinical. Just like all
            those little lists and run-books he had written himself for work. He
            was usually great in emergencies, so it struck him as odd that right
            now, when it most mattered, he couldn&apos;t think. Those bullet
            points didn&apos;t look as small as they used to.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;You&apos;re handling this well,&quot; the nurse reassured him
            in a low voice. &quot;Do you think you can manage this?&quot;
          </p>
          <p>
            John looked at her and then the list, folded it once, then twice,
            and then again until it became an accordion of individual tasks. It
            was something to do. Having things to do would make him feel like he
            was helping. He nodded and gave her a small smile.
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;Now, go get &apos;em,&quot; she pushed him slightly.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            John went home. He got the laptops, put in the medical leave in
            Jayce&apos;s work calendar, and marked himself off for the next few
            days. He cleaned out the food that had gone cold on the kitchen
            counter and put it away in the fridge, deciding he would think about
            food safety when he had a clearer head. He&apos;d hate to waste food
            unnecessarily. He took a shower and prepared the away bag. He then
            went to Jayce&apos;s place. It was as lifeless as it had always
            been. Except for the wall of books, it might as well have been a
            hotel room. You&apos;d expect people&apos;s houses to match the
            owner&apos;s personality, but in Jayce&apos;s case, there was a
            discrepancy John always found unsettling. It was probably why they
            mostly spent time at his place. Not that they were far from each
            other. As he was packing a few changes of clothes for Jayce, he
            chuckled at how similar this was to packing for a holiday. That
            chuckle broke the tension that was keeping all of John&apos;s
            feelings from getting out. He started sobbing uncontrollably as he
            hugged one of Jayce&apos;s jumpers.
          </p>
          <Separator>{"\u273C"}</Separator>
          <p>
            John all but ran to the hospital and went straight for the intensive
            care unit. He didn&apos;t know exactly what he was expecting to see.
            Maybe an awake Jayce, now completely bald, already cracking jokes as
            if nothing happened. However, reality was a bit different. The
            Intensive care unit was busy, and it lacked privacy. Most patients
            were unconscious, including Jayce, and wires and machines tracked
            their every vital. He wasn&rsquo;t bald, just shaved on the left
            side of his head where the dressing covered his wound. The rest of
            his hair was still intact, swiped to the side. He looked like he
            could walk out onto some punk rock catwalk.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            John sat down on Jayce&apos;s left side and stared. He wanted to
            touch him, feel his warmth, to make sure he was real. But fear held
            him back. What if he did something wrong and harmed his recovery?
            So, he just stared instead. He took in every single detail of his
            face and watched as his eyelashes slightly fluttered as he breathed.
            Watching him brought back some calm as their breaths began to sync.
          </p>
          <p>
            A different nurse came to check on Jayce. Despite his moving around,
            John&apos;s eyes did not move from Jayce for a second. There were
            rules about visitors being quiet in the ICU, but the nurse thought
            he was taking it further than any visitor he&apos;d ever seen in the
            room. Not one word was uttered. If he wasn&apos;t breathing,
            you&apos;d think him a statue. The nurse came to check in a few more
            times, but John had not moved an inch. He was used to rude visitors
            not acknowledging him, which usually annoyed him, but something
            about this one felt different. The first few times, he&apos;d
            decided not to say anything. Why disturb a perfectly peaceful shift?
            But every time he ignored John, something gnawed at him.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>&quot;The surgery went well...&quot;</p>
          <p>
            John turned around to face the nurse, startled. He had not noticed
            someone else there. The combination of surprise and apology followed
            by confusion on John&apos;s face brought a smile to the nurse&apos;s
            lips. John had a very transparent face, and there wasn&apos;t an
            ounce of rudeness in it.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;I said, the surgery went well. But we won&apos;t know until he
            wakes up. I&apos;ll keep monitoring him, and when he wakes up, I
            will call for the doctor. He&apos;ll be groggy at first, so
            don&apos;t worry too much. If he wakes up when I am not here, can
            you press that blue button by the bed? The one saying nurse. Not the
            red one. You&apos;re not colour blind, are you?&quot;
          </p>
          <p>John shook his head.&nbsp;</p>
          <p>
            &quot;You know, he&apos;s not going to wake up any faster if you
            stare at him like that...&quot; the nurse added as he left.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            That thought hadn&apos;t even occurred to John. Watching
            Jayce&apos;s rhythmic breaths had brought a sense of calm and
            timelessness. He realised he could do it forever. It made the
            waiting more bearable, as it didn&apos;t feel like waiting. Jayce
            waking up was the more terrifying thought. Right now, he was
            sleeping. But he might wake up only to find out things went wrong.
            Then, a more terrifying thought crept in. What if he never woke up?
          </p>
          <p>
            Fortunately, John&apos;s anxiety spiral did not last long, as Jayce
            opened his eyes. John&apos;s every thought stopped for a moment.
            Then his hand immediately reached for the blue button. He stood up
            to be in Jayce&apos;s field of vision and waited with bated breath.
          </p>
          <Separator>{"\u273C"}</Separator>
          <p>
            Jayce was thinking about the whiskey he had had at the business
            meeting before, wondering if it would negatively impact the
            procedure. Then, suddenly, he was staring at a white ceiling. His
            head was throbbing, and the left side of his head was itchy as hell.
            He tried to lift his hand to scratch, but it was as if it was moving
            through water against the current. His hand wobbled and after a few
            tries, he gave up. Slowly, the dots started to connect. He&apos;d
            had brain surgery. A shadow loomed over him, but he could tell
            nothing more due to the lack of focus in his vision. Squinting only
            gave him more of a headache. The shadow didn&apos;t move, so he let
            it come into focus on its own. Slowly, John&apos;s features became
            clearer. His haggard face was lined with worry. Jayce tried to
            smile, yet his face responded as if he was in somebody else&apos;s
            body. Worry was not an emotion he&apos;d ever seen on John&apos;s
            face before today. Well, he wasn&apos;t even sure if it was today
            anymore. His face twitched, then jerked awkwardly into a half smile.
            He knew he&apos;d succeeded when John&apos;s face lit up like a kid
            at Christmas. Jayce thought back to when they first met and how
            ridiculous it was to have thought John was expressionless. The man
            was more transparent than a thinly stretched piece of cling film.
          </p>
          <p>
            The doctor came in, and John moved out of the way. He would have
            much preferred to keep looking at John&apos;s face. Not that the
            doctor was particularly ugly to look at, but it was understandable
            that he didn&apos;t really want to see her. He tried to turn his
            head to the left to see them both, instinct taking over. Sharp,
            searing pain shot through his skull. His breath caught. A hand
            steadied him, guiding him back. His bed started to move with a jolt,
            bringing him to a more upright position.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            As the ring of the pain in his head subsided, he was able to hear
            the Doctor&apos;s voice. She shone a light in his eyes from left to
            right. He assumed she wanted him to follow it, but she could have
            just asked. She then said something else. But it wasn&apos;t right.
            Did she say he had chimpanzees? Why was she talking about yarn
            jumping in dollhouses? He looked at John. Was the doctor insane? But
            there was no confusion on his face. Wait, was it just him? Did the
            surgery fail? He could hear her talking, and there was a growing
            look of expectation on both of their faces. Was he supposed to do
            something? The light in the eyes was a test, wasn&apos;t it? Had he
            passed it? He tried to say that he didn&apos;t understand them, but
            all that came out was:
          </p>
          <p>&quot;No exfoliate.&quot;</p>
          <p>
            He was making less sense than they were. Something was wrong. The
            beeping of the heart monitor kept getting faster. A feeling of dread
            sunk deep within his chest.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            The doctor pointed at her ear and then at herself, as she said,
            &quot;Will cup him?&quot;.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            He thought she was trying to mime &apos;Can you hear me?&apos;, but
            that was definitely not what she was saying. He nodded slightly,
            afraid to move too much in case his head hurt again.
          </p>
          <p>
            He tried to move his right hand, and he found it even weaker than
            his left had been earlier. But it felt different. If his left hand
            moved as if he was drunk, his right felt downright as if it was made
            out of lead. Feeling constricted made him want to move more. Trying
            to move his left, the movement came more easily. His hand drifted
            towards John, but he withdrew as he remembered John was not
            comfortable with physical touch. To his surprise, before he could
            move away, John grabbed his hand and squeezed it. He squeezed it
            back. John smiled. Did he do something right? It felt like he
            had.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            The doctor mimed some movements to him, and he figured out he had to
            repeat them. Some were easier than others. At no point did John let
            go of his hand, but he wasn&apos;t complaining. It was warm and
            soft. He didn&apos;t know if John realised it, but his thumb
            continuously caressed his hand. It felt too nice to tell him about
            it... Well, not that he could... The doctor&apos;s attention focused
            on John. Jayce glanced between John and the doctor, but her words
            blurred together in an indecipherable hum. He would think he could
            understand a word here and there, only for its meaning to escape
            him. As John listened, his caresses would occasionally stop, and a
            crease would form between his eyes. Then, he would squeeze his hand
            and resume.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>&quot;Sit cute fishing flower pot how?&quot; he asked.</p>
          <p>
            The doctor met his eyes with a sad look while John squeezed his
            hand. Or was it disappointment? The doctor pulled out a stack of
            pamphlets and handed them to John, who took them with his free hand.
            Then, she just left. John pulled the chair closer with his leg and
            sat down, not once letting go of Jayce&apos;s hand.
          </p>
          <p>
            Now that he&apos;d heard John&apos;s voice, even if for a brief
            moment, Jayce longed to hear it again. But he appreciated the
            silence. Because he was starting to suspect he was no longer able to
            communicate. He hated that John was finally speaking, but by some
            twisted curse of destiny, he was unable to understand him. He may
            never be able to understand him again. Well, they&apos;d gotten
            along just fine before when John didn&apos;t say a word. But
            he&apos;d filled in the gaps for both of them. It was definitely
            karma. Karma for talking too much. His eyelids felt heavy. He had
            just woken up, so how could he be tired? He wanted nothing more than
            to have a conversation with John, but even that failed attempt at
            talking and moving a few limbs left him more exhausted than he felt
            after pulling an all-nighter. His eyes closed on their own, and he
            drifted to sleep.
          </p>
          <Separator>{"\u273C"}</Separator>
          <p>
            Jayce woke up once to see John sleeping in a chair, a little bit of
            drool forming at the edge of his mouth. Jayce tried to move a
            little, only to feel a little resistance in his left hand. John was
            still holding it. He stopped so as not to wake him. He closed his
            eyes and drifted back to sleep.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <Separator>{"\u273C"}</Separator>
          <p>
            In the two days Jayce was in the intensive care unit, regardless
            when he&apos;d wake up, John would be there. It didn&apos;t matter
            the time. It was as if he had never left his side. He knew he must
            have left at some point or another, at least to go to the bathroom,
            and hopefully to eat too. Eating was not allowed in the ICU, so he
            hoped John would eat while he was sleeping, yet at the same time, he
            wouldn&apos;t put it past John to just forget. Jayce had never
            pegged John for a clingy person. If he were to describe him, it
            would be like a cat; always quiet, generally there, but keeping its
            distance, and occasionally it would bring you a dead mouse to show
            you it cared about you. Then again, you would hear of those stories
            of when their humans got ill, cats would come and sleep and purr on
            top of them until they got better. He could almost picture it as he
            looked at John, the cat ears, trying to look aloof but sitting
            beside him, not moving. His imagination drifted to the image of John
            climbing up onto his lap and beginning to purr. Laughter escaped
            him. John&apos;s eyebrow rose in his direction, but there was a soft
            smile on his face. Jayce doubted that smile would remain there if he
            were to describe why he was laughing, so he just shrugged and
            smiled.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            That was how it had always been with John, they didn&apos;t need
            words to communicate. Well, John didn&apos;t need them. Jayce did.
            But for a moment, in the quiet, things felt normal. Even if just for
            a moment. But the nurses would come in, as would the doctors, and it
            just served as a reminder of how different things were. He would
            sometimes think he&apos;d pick up on a word, but what use was it
            even if he did get it correctly when he couldn&rsquo;t understand
            anything else. The thought that he might remain like this forever
            weighed on him. It was only the optimistic looks John would give him
            that kept him going. But even the shortest interaction would leave
            him drained, even if he couldn&apos;t understand a word. If it was
            just him and John, he managed to stay awake in the silence longer.
            Eventually, sleep would take him.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <Separator>{"\u273C"}</Separator>
          <p>
            Jayce was moved out of the ICU to a private room after 2 days. The
            place was quieter, and he found he was able to sleep more soundly,
            which increased the stretches of time he could stay awake. At times,
            Jayce felt like his understanding was becoming better, only to get
            stuck not understanding anything again. So, he spent his time awake
            in silence with John. Jayce was not one to stand still. He was never
            comfortable with doing nothing or just thinking in silence. He moved
            around the room, pacing back and forth. It was awkward at first, but
            at least he felt like he was making progress. Whatever that meant.
            When he woke up from his afternoon nap, something he hadn&apos;t
            done since he was a baby, John had set up a game of Jenga on the
            small table in the room. It was a simple game that required no
            words, so they started playing. John had the advantage on this one,
            but Jayce was competitive. The frustration of losing only fuelled
            him further.
          </p>
          <p>
            The doctor came to visit for the first time during their fifth game
            of Jenga. She watched the game for a little while. Jayce hadn&apos;t
            noticed her presence as he was too focused on getting that one last
            brick that would likely win him the game. As the tower collapsed, he
            finally noticed her.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            She began to talk, and the words came across jumbled and confusing.
            However, Jayce managed to latch onto a few known words like
            &quot;better&quot; and &quot;home&quot;, maybe. Then came the
            others: &quot;sheep&quot;, &quot;elevator&quot;, &quot;wrap&quot;,
            &quot;subway&quot;- absolute nonsense. His heart sank. The quiet
            game of Jenga almost made him forget his reality, and the struggle
            to understand brought it all crashing back. Was he even
            misunderstanding the ones he thought he&apos;d gotten?
          </p>
          <p>
            Her face looked like she was delivering good news. But when he
            looked at John, he saw another expression he&apos;d never seen
            before. His brow was furrowed. He spoke. Jayce could not distinguish
            the words, but every word was delivered slowly and deliberately. Not
            a single word was raised, yet the doctor shrank before his gaze. He
            had never seen this side of John before, and he was glad for it.
            Being on the other end of it would have probably left Jayce
            crumbling. It was a wonder that the doctor was not running for the
            door already. He&apos;d thought he&apos;d seen him angry before,
            occasionally when some problem escaped him, and he&apos;d furrow his
            brow and grumble rudely at the computer screen. But no, this was a
            completely different beast. What did the doctor say that would
            elicit such a reaction? It had sounded to Jayce like it was good
            news... He may not be able to understand speech, but he thought he
            could still manage people&apos;s body language.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            The exchange between the doctor and John lasted for a few minutes
            longer. Then she left. John was shaking. He went to his bag, took
            out an iPad, and drew on it. Two stick figures, an arrow, and a
            house. So... they were going home. Surely, that was good news...
            Then it hit him. He still couldn&apos;t understand a full sentence,
            and producing even one word correctly escaped him completely. John
            handed Jayce a set of ironed, freshly laundered clothes. Jayce went
            to change, his mind spiralling... He thought he was doing better; he
            almost defeated John at Jenga. And a few days ago, he could hardly
            move his right hand. Did that mean he would just get better in time
            or that he would be stuck at this for the rest of his life? The
            thought crashed through him. What kind of life would that be? Where
            he couldn&apos;t understand anyone, and no one would be able to
            understand him. He sat on the floor, hugging his knees and started
            sobbing; then, the sobs turned to outright wailing. There was a
            knock on the door, then the door opened. John came in and knelt in
            front of a hyperventilating Jayce. Gently, John wiped the tears from
            his cheeks, maintaining full eye contact. Jayce wanted to stop the
            undignified sobbing but couldn&apos;t, so he hid his face in
            John&apos;s chest, who calmly patted his head.
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;Science... fiction... is... Recipe... soul... has...
            book&quot;, he said in between sobs &quot;Forest... to... cave...
            star... make... beer? Carrot... which?&quot;
          </p>
          <Separator>{"\u273C"}</Separator>
          <p>
            John helped Jayce get dressed and took him home after he calmed down
            a little bit. It wasn&apos;t that he had started to feel better, but
            he had run out of tears and grown too tired to continue. They got in
            a taxi, and Jayce fell asleep on John&apos;s shoulder. It was a
            short sleep, but when he got out he realised they were in front of
            John&apos;s building. He wanted to protest, but he was too tired. He
            could have walked to his place; it was only ten minutes away, but he
            didn&apos;t have the energy. He would do it tomorrow.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            They went up to the flat. When they entered, John bent down to help
            him with his shoes. Jayce bent down to take over, but moving so fast
            made him feel so dizzy that he immediately regretted his decision.
            Yet, he tried not to let it show. He should at least do his own
            laces.
          </p>
          <p>&quot;Goose string would. Penguin no.&quot;, he mumbled.&nbsp;</p>
          <p>
            For once, it didn&apos;t matter if he didn&apos;t make sense. He
            just wanted to mumble something to show some level of
            dissatisfaction. But he followed John obediently afterwards to the
            bedroom. John left a set of pyjamas on the bed for him and took a
            few items for himself, then left the room to give him some privacy.
            After changing, he sat at the edge of the bed. He&apos;d slept on
            John&apos;s couch a few times before. Not his proudest moments, yet
            John would always let him. However, John&apos;s bedroom was always
            off limits. It felt like being allowed into the inner sanctum. He
            hadn&apos;t realised just how many books John had. Even if
            you&apos;d been so close to someone for so long, there were always
            new things to learn about them.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            The hobby suited him, but it made him wonder what else he
            didn&apos;t know about his closest friend. He tried to focus on the
            spines of the books, but he might as well have stared at an
            AI-generated image of a bookcase, for there was nothing on there
            that made sense. Exhausted, he crashed onto the bed. And, wow, that
            mattress was comfortable, as were the sheets, and the pillows. It
            was like lying on a cloud. Another thing he learned about John: he
            had expensive taste in beds and bed linen.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            John returned soon after with a tray. On it, a bowl of congee, a
            large glass of water, and a wide selection of pills in different
            colours. John watched him as he ate. When John stood up to leave and
            take the tray, Jayce grabbed his arm. John stopped and looked at the
            tray, but Jayce did not budge. There was not much strength in
            Jayce&apos;s grip, and if John wanted to get away, he could have.
            John stood there staring at Jayce, then at the tray, then again at
            Jayce... A sigh escaped him, and his arm slipped from Jayce&apos;s
            hold. Then he was gone. Disappeared alongside the tray. Jayce stared
            at the hands on his lap and laughed at himself. The sense of loss he
            felt as soon as John left the room took over him so deeply it felt
            unbearable. He had wanted to go to his place, but he couldn&apos;t
            stand being alone even just to sleep now.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            Pure exhilaration filled him when John returned just a minute or so
            later. He hadn&apos;t even realised he was holding his breath until
            it had escaped him in a shaky exhale. He reached for John&apos;s arm
            and tugged gently, at which point John sat on the bed next to him.
            Then he tugged at him again, with more certainty. John&apos;s
            fingers twitched, and he pulled away slightly. But then, with a
            sigh, he climbed into bed next to Jayce. There was some discomfort
            on John&apos;s face, so Jayce kept his distance for a moment.
            Sitting there, in silence, filled him with a sense of normalcy. He
            had liked the sound of John&apos;s voice, but it was too painful to
            be able to hear it and not understand what he was saying. This was
            comforting, familiar. They stayed like that for a few minutes.
          </p>
          <p>
            Then, Jayce pushed his luck. Slowly, he wrapped his arms around
            John&apos;s waist, pressing his face into his side. John tensed up.
            For a moment, Jayce thought he&apos;d finally overstepped. But
            before he could let John go, instead of pulling away, John relaxed
            into his embrace. Then, just as slowly, a hand drifted to
            Jayce&apos;s back. Jayce relaxed further into the hug, all
            apprehension washed away. John&apos;s other hand brushed away a few
            loose strands of hair from Jayce&apos;s forehead, and they settled
            into a silent moment together. He wanted to enjoy it longer, but in
            the cloud-like bed and John&apos;s warm embrace, sleep took him.
          </p>
          <Separator>{"\u273C"}</Separator>
          <p>
            John woke Jayce up in the morning to give him his meds. The side of
            the bed where John had sat down the previous night was still warm.
            Jayce rejoiced in the knowledge that John had stayed with him
            through the night. His eyelids were heavy, and his body felt like a
            ton of bricks if he tried to stand up. The bed was too comfortable
            and inviting, and sleep took him again. Jayce awoke around midday to
            the sound of a printer. He was not aware that John had a printer. At
            the company, there was a policy of no printed documents, not even
            PDFs, that John had been adamant about. He&apos;d been fairly
            easygoing on most matters, but this had been one of a few instances
            where John never budged. If his brain made a mess of words, did it
            do the same to sounds?
          </p>
          <p>
            He slowly got up from bed. He&apos;d realised that if he got up too
            fast, he&apos;d be dizzy. Then quietly went to the living room. Sure
            enough, there was John. While a printer was running, he was chopping
            up paper with a set of large scissors. Jayce approached slowly, but
            John was too focused on his task to notice. Whenever John was
            focused on a task, he had this adorable habit of squinting his eyes
            just a little and sticking his tongue ever so slightly out of the
            corner of his mouth. He was cutting up cards. Jayce reached over
            John&apos;s shoulder when he put down the scissors to get the next
            sheet of paper from the printer. John jumped away in surprise but
            let Jayce look at the cards. They were business card-sized and
            shaped, and on them there were photos.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            Each card had a photo and some writing on it. He picked one up.
            There was a bad selfie of John and some letters. He knew the letters
            must have spelt John, but his brain refused to make sense of them.
            Instead, the letters jumped around and refused to act nicely,
            mocking him. A headache started to form as he tried to will them to
            behave, so he gave up with a sigh. John picked up a card with a
            photo of Jayce, then shuffled through the papers in the printer, and
            pointed at a photo of a box wrapped with a bow, a present, and then
            a stylised drawing of a mouth with arrows going out, and an ear with
            arrows going in. A gift for Jayce, to speak and hear. The words
            attached to the images still jumped around mockingly, but the images
            were clear, as was their meaning.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            Jayce pointed at the scissors in John&apos;s hand and showed him an
            open palm. He wasn&apos;t an invalid, he could at the very least cut
            up his communication aids... John hesitated, and Jayce frowned.
            John&apos;s watch vibrated. Jayce used the distraction to grab the
            scissors and a few sheets of card paper, then went and sat on the
            sofa. A sigh escaped John, but he relented. He stood up, left, and
            came back with a tray of medicine for Jayce, who took them without
            question. For about an hour, Jayce slowly cut the cards and
            organised them into categories. There were two of each. He assumed
            one for himself and one for John. Why hadn&apos;t Jayce thought of
            doing this for John when he couldn&apos;t talk? Then the question
            hit him. Was he unable to talk, or did he just choose not to talk
            all this time? He brushed away the thought and focused on the
            cutting. However, after an hour, he struggled to keep his eyes open
            and fell asleep on the sofa. He woke up only later in the evening,
            an hour before dinner, in bed. John must have carried him.
          </p>
          <Separator>{"\u273C"}</Separator>
          <p>
            &nbsp;Over the next couple of days, John placed post-it notes with
            the names on every single item in the house on them. Jayce knew,
            logically, that they were meant to help, but they were just a
            reminder of how far he still had to go. Occasionally, when he was
            supposed to be sleeping, he&apos;d catch John speaking on the phone.
            While he couldn&apos;t tell what he was saying, it was the same
            angry voice he had at the hospital with the doctor. As soon as
            he&apos;d realise Jayce was awake, he would put the phone away and
            change his demeanour altogether.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            Jayce could feel something was wrong, that something was bothering
            John. Not knowing what it was only made him feel a lot worse. Should
            he have been able to understand by now, or talk? It struck him that
            other than the obvious, he didn&apos;t actually know what happened
            to him. No wonder John was treating him like a child. He hadn&apos;t
            even bothered to find out what had happened to him. It was
            understandable since he couldn&apos;t be communicated with... but it
            left him feeling helpless nonetheless. He just took the meds John
            gave him and allowed himself to become a burden. While John
            hadn&apos;t taken any holiday that year, using it on him, felt
            wrong. Especially considering how hard he usually worked. He was
            taking care of Jayce, plotting weird little games to help him, spent
            time researching stuff, cooked, and probably a lot more stuff behind
            the scenes that Jayce wasn&apos;t even aware of. And Jayce slept so
            much he wasn&apos;t of any. He cursed himself for being
            useless.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            He stared at the cup of tea in his hands. John had stepped out for a
            little while. He looked at the floor and thought he could at least
            vacuum, even though there was nothing really to vacuum. He felt
            sluggish and while he wanted to, he felt unable to move. He looked
            back at the cup, it said cup on it. Wait a moment. It said&nbsp;
            <em>cup</em>&nbsp;on it! Jayce looked away and then back at the cup.
            It still said&nbsp;<em>cup</em>&nbsp;on it! Joy bubbled up inside
            him and he jumped up, spilling some of the hot tea on himself and
            the floor.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            He quickly put the cup on the table and grabbed the box of tissues
            to dab the tea before it stained. The writing on the box was as
            jumbled as it had been before. If the joy of reading cup made him
            feel that good, the drop of not being able to read the tissue box
            affected him even harder. It was just that, one silly 3 letter word.
            That&apos;s all he could understand. Toddlers would likely do better
            than him. That brief moment of extreme joy just as quickly
            transformed into despair. That one word that gave him hope made him
            feel even worse when the hope was taken away. He curled up on the
            floor, hugging his knees. He wished John was there to comfort him.
            He hated himself for depending on John so much, that he
            couldn&apos;t handle being alone for even a little while. He grabbed
            the throw from the sofa and wrapped himself in it, sinking further
            into the ground, willing himself to disappear from the world for a
            while.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            That was how John found him. He just came and sat next to him
            without a word, their knees touching. Jayce wanted to hug him and
            forget about the world but didn&apos;t allow himself to. He wanted
            to tell John to go away, and again he couldn&apos;t. If he was
            honest with himself, he appreciated his presence. John didn&apos;t
            move until Jayce came out of the blanket on his own. Jayce pointed
            at the cup and tried to say it, however it came out as&nbsp;
            <em>goose</em>. John nodded and smiled. Sitting next to John and his
            encouraging smile, gave Jayce a little motivation. He went and
            grabbed a notepad and some markers and drew the image of a brain. He
            was somewhat pleased it looked as good as he usually drew. He then
            took the drawing to John, handed him the markers, and pointed at the
            drawing and then his head. He didn&apos;t want to feel helpless
            anymore. John would have to explain.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            John took the pens and notebook, and scratched his head. He was
            thinking. He went and grabbed some of the cards, and then sat at the
            dining room table. Jayce followed. He then placed the speaking card
            on the front left side of the brain and the hearing card on the
            middle left side of the brain. Just to be sure, he put an image of a
            pen on the front and one of a book on the lateral side, likely to
            mean it&apos;s not only for speaking and listening but reading and
            writing too. John took the cards away and circled the two areas.
            Then he got lost in thought for a while. He took the red marker and
            then drew a big explosion-like spiky circle on the frontal area and
            a small bit of red on the lateral area. He drew the cards for the
            doctor and the clock. Jayce waited for more, but there was no
            more.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            The explanation answered some questions but created even more, and
            he had no idea how to ask them. Despite his attempts to put on a
            brave face, Jayce&apos;s shoulders were slumped with defeat. He
            bowed his head in thanks, took the drawing, and while staring at it,
            trudged along to the bedroom. He lay there staring at the red blobs
            on his brain. His imagination was working overtime on all of the
            worst-case scenarios. John stayed next to him for the rest of the
            night, trying to hide his concern. But once you knew John, his poker
            face was worse than non-existent. And Jayce knew John. While Jayce
            appreciated it, it only made him feel worse.
          </p>
          <Separator>{"\u273C"}</Separator>
          <p>
            The next couple of days passed in a blur. Jayce only felt more
            discouraged as he stared at the writing. A few more words were
            starting to stay put, but he was growing too terrified of getting
            his hopes up. If his understanding of John&apos;s explanation was
            right, then even if he could understand those words, it didn&apos;t
            mean he would ever be able to speak again. What was the point, then?
            Jayce always prided himself on being able to talk with just about
            anyone about just about anything. All they needed to do was listen,
            even if they didn&apos;t, he&apos;d keep talking until they
            did.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            John had been spending more time in the kitchen lately. Jayce
            wondered if he was avoiding him. It had become an understanding
            between them that Jayce would never enter the kitchen while John was
            there. He had tried to help once when John was preparing dinner and
            the sauce ended up being so salty he had to down a whole litre of
            water afterwards. While he tried to redeem himself by practising at
            home, and even brought some successful brownies he had made, the
            rule remained. He could do the dishes afterwards, but while John was
            in the kitchen he was never to disturb. That had felt normal in the
            past, but with everything going on, it felt more and more like he
            was leeching on John&apos;s good will. He considered leaving, but as
            much as he hated it, he was dependent on John. It was a terrible
            feeling to be dependent on someone. And the way John didn&apos;t
            even seem to mind, made him feel conflicted.
          </p>
          <p>
            That evening, he woke up from his afternoon nap to the smell of food
            filling the house. He was still drowsy, as he always seemed to be
            when waking up nowadays. Even if he had slept for ten or even twelve
            hours. The smell felt familiar, like a distant memory, but he
            couldn&apos;t quite place it. It did fill him with a sense of
            warmth, and nostalgia, however. He wanted to peek into the kitchen
            to find out what was going on, but he abided by the rule. His old
            seat at the dining table was the one seat where he could catch a
            glimpse of John inside the kitchen, but he still couldn&apos;t bring
            himself to sit there again. He sat at the table, trying to rack his
            brain for what the smell reminded him of. Had the stroke affected
            his memory too? John hadn&apos;t said anything about that, but he
            couldn&apos;t dismiss it, could he... He felt like he thought
            exactly as before, but when people are drunk they still think they
            are perfectly sober, most of the time. So he wouldn&apos;t be the
            best judge of that, either.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            He wanted to give John his space, but curiosity got the better of
            him. As long as he didn&apos;t step past the door to the kitchen, he
            would be OK, right? From the doorway, he watched John as he wrapped
            dumplings. A few lumps of dough were crumpled on the table, evidence
            of failed past attempts. He wasn&apos;t making just any dumplings,
            he was making soup dumplings. They were his favourite as a child.
            His grandma would make them for him whenever he was really down, or
            when he had something to celebrate. After his grandmother passed, he
            begged his mother to make them, but they took too much time, and his
            mother was not a patient person. She did end up making a couple
            giant ones for him for his acceptance to university, but the ratio
            of dough to filling was off, and some were not able to hold the
            soup.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            Even now, he would go for soup dumplings whenever he had something
            to celebrate, even if they didn&apos;t taste quite the same. Among
            all the things John was already doing for him, he was making him
            soup dumplings, well known for how difficult they were to make. What
            had he done to deserve this? His debt to John was growing larger
            every day. He didn&apos;t want to be ungrateful, but this was
            starting to weigh on him. After a while, John noticed him and tried
            to hide what he was doing, but it was too late. John acted like
            he&apos;d been caught red-handed doing something wrong, yet Jayce
            felt like he was the one in the wrong for putting him through so
            much trouble.&nbsp;&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            Old Jayce may have teased John a little, noting his embarrassment
            upon being discovered, but this Jayce didn&apos;t have the words. As
            John ushered him to the dining room table, he followed obediently
            and sat down. He mentally prepared himself for the dumplings. Jayce
            had been to every single restaurant in the city that served soup
            dumplings, to the point he had obtained quite the reputation within
            the very niche community of restaurant reviewers specializing in
            soup dumplings. He prepared himself for the worst. Now there was
            nothing that John had ever cooked that tasted bad, except maybe that
            one experimental dish Jayce tasted despite John trying to stop him.
            So correction, there was nothing that John ever cooked and served
            that tasted bad. But these were difficult dumplings to get fully
            right. And he needed to not show any disappointment, as it was the
            effort that really mattered and he did appreciate it. The food
            critic had to go on a temporary holiday.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            John came through with a steamer basket full of dumplings, placed
            them in front of Jayce and waited with bated breath. Jayce took a
            dumpling, and slowly, but surely gave it a taste. The world stopped
            for him at that moment. They tasted exactly like the ones his
            grandmother used to make. Warmth filled him to his very core. How
            was that even possible? He rushed for the second dumpling and ate it
            so quickly, the hot soup burned his mouth, but he didn&apos;t care.
            They were perfect. Yes, they were all slightly different shapes and
            sizes, but, as his grandma used to say, sometimes we need to add a
            little imperfection to show the care that was put into making the
            dumpling. However, he always felt like it was just something she
            told him so he wouldn&apos;t feel bad about not being able to fold
            the dumplings quite right. But now he understood, he could see John
            in the shapes of the dumplings. He stared at John, one question
            floating in the air between them - how?
          </p>
          <p>
            John pulled two cards. A phone and the flag of Singapore. He&apos;d
            called his mother. That meant his mother knew he had a stroke. He
            felt a heavy weight in his stomach as his breath caught. Was his
            mother coming over? He could already hear her berating him for
            inconveniencing John. Looking at his progress report and seeing
            none, then&nbsp;&nbsp;tssking in disappointment. Well, actually
            here&apos;s a good thing about the stroke for once, she&apos;d say
            all she&apos;d want to say, and he wouldn&apos;t be able to
            understand a word. Wouldn&apos;t that be great? He looked at John,
            trying to figure out how to ask the question he dreaded the answer
            to. He picked the Singapore flag, a plane, and a photo of
            John&apos;s home. Would that be enough for him to understand? It
            appeared so. As John shook his head, relief flooded Jayce. What kind
            of person was he? To dread his mother coming to take care of him so
            much, yet so comfortable inconveniencing his friend like this?
          </p>
          <p>
            John did not let him ponder on that too long as he pushed more
            dumplings onto his plate. The dumplings still had their magic power.
            They could make any shitty situation not feel bad. And Chez John
            officially had the best soup dumplings in the whole of London. He
            wanted to thank John, but he didn&apos;t know how. Based on the
            satisfied look on John&apos;s face, he somehow seemed to get the
            message anyway.
          </p>
          <p>&nbsp;</p>
          <Separator>{"\u273C"}</Separator>
          <p>
            The day after the dumplings, John had to return to work. Maybe the
            dumplings hadn&apos;t been just a way to cheer Jayce up, but an
            advance apology. John started by taking meetings and working from
            home. Jayce couldn&apos;t help but eavesdrop on the meetings. He
            couldn&apos;t understand anything, and that was even more
            frustrating for him. It was clear that John was picking up the slack
            for him. Yet all he could feel was resentment that he was taking
            over his job. And then, he just felt more like a horrible person
            because of it. Despite it not being true, it still felt like
            taunting him for the things he could not do.&nbsp;&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            The thing that annoyed him even more, is that he&apos;d throw
            tantrums like a child, puffing up his cheeks and everything, and
            John didn&apos;t even mind. It was almost as if he found it amusing.
            True, Jayce didn&apos;t think it was the right way to behave either,
            but it made him feel better. He felt guilty, yet did it anyway.
            During quiet times, he felt like he was making progress. He was
            starting to be able to read the words on the post-it notes around
            the house more easily. He would even pick up the singular no or yes
            as they came up in conversation, but when he tried to focus on
            John&apos;s calls he&apos;d only get tired, and some of the progress
            he&apos;d made on the individual words even disappeared.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            Things were moving too slowly for his comfort. He wanted to at least
            be able to understand what was going on around him. Maybe at least
            that way he could be a little bit more useful, more self-sufficient.
            He could still draw, so maybe he could do an hour or two of design
            work, if he knew what he was doing.&nbsp;&nbsp;The more he
            struggled, the harder it got. And the harder it got, the more
            depressed Jayce started to feel.
          </p>
          <Separator>{"\u273C"}</Separator>
          <p>
            John started going into the office more often. While he&apos;d
            prepare Jayce&apos;s meals and medicine for while he was gone, that
            was not what Jayce missed the most. He felt like he was able to
            focus more on being able to read individual words. But he missed
            John&apos;s presence. As the time came for dinner, he found himself
            sitting by the door like a puppy waiting for his owner to arrive. He
            would tidy the house regularly, but soon the routine seemed to lose
            its purpose. Sometimes, he&apos;d wake up and just lie in bed
            staring at the ceiling, having no will to move. Eventually, the only
            thing that would get him to get up would be thinking of John getting
            back home and finding him still in bed.
          </p>
          <p>
            One day, John came back carrying a bag full of books and handed them
            to Jayce, who was lying on the sofa, waiting.
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;Read,&quot; he said with a smile. One single word, and Jayce
            got it. Or so he hoped.
          </p>
          <p>
            Inside the bag there was a collection of action manhwas. They had a
            lot of pictures, so he would get the gist of what was being said,
            most of the time. Because of the genre, there wasn&apos;t that much
            dialogue overall, so it was meant to be easy. It made sense as a
            next step.&nbsp;&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            John retreated to the kitchen. As guilty as Jayce felt about him
            working, while picking up the slack for Jayce too, and then also
            doing the cooking, Jayce let him be. The kitchen was a space that
            John had to himself to be alone and decompress after a day at work.
            Trying to change that would only be in his service, not John&apos;s.
            He&apos;d tried to surprise John after he went to the office for the
            first time by cooking, but the only surprise was that the salt had
            been replaced with sugar, as he misread the label. He was confident
            he would be able to do a better job now, but he was too nervous to
            try again.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            He picked up the action manhwa, and started to flip through the
            pages. The art was gorgeous, and he recognised it as an older one he
            had read a long time ago on his commute to work. He remembered the
            overall plot. While the dialogue escaped him, this was surely going
            to be something easy enough.&nbsp;&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            He tried. He caught a word, it was an SFX, but he could read it. A
            satisfied smile crossed his face. He tried some more, just one short
            line of dialogue. He genuinely tried. But the more he tried, the
            harder it got, and even the single separate words started to play
            tricks on him. He picked up another one, but the words kept mocking
            him. He even avoided the beginnings where there was a more
            considerable exposition, but as he tried to fit the words he
            remembered, and they did not match what was on the paper, it just
            became ever more frustrating. Tension built up first in his throat,
            then it spread through his body, until he could bear it no more and
            threw the book at the wall.&nbsp;&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            By the time he realised what he had done, it was too late. The book
            flew past John&apos;s face and missed him by a millimetre. The look
            on John&apos;s face was not just one of surprise, there was panic in
            his eyes. They both stood frozen for a few seconds, after which John
            broke the standstill by just walking out the door. Walking
            wasn&apos;t quite the right word for it, he moved as fast as the
            layout of the flat allowed him to, short of breaking into a run. The
            door to the flat slammed behind John with a thud, similar to the
            thud in Jayce&apos;s chest as the events that unfolded before him
            were processed by his brain.&nbsp;&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            As he stared at the door, part of him hoped that John would come
            back any second, the other thought he had lost him for good. He
            messed up. He&apos;d let his anger out and ruined the best, no, only
            true friendship he had. The man had taken care of him, better than
            his family ever would. He put up with his whims, and stupid
            behaviours. All with a kindness he didn&apos;t deserve. On the
            bright side, he managed to prove it to John. He didn&apos;t deserve
            his kindness. It was good that he left, it was the appropriate
            response. He thought he should probably go to his own home. What a
            right bastard he was, chasing the man out of his own home. And to
            prove just how much of a bastard he was, he could not bring himself
            to stand on his own two feet and leave. He stood there curled in on
            himself, willing himself to move, but instead just digging his nails
            deeper into his arms. He felt like crying, but he didn&apos;t
            deserve that, he didn&apos;t deserve to be sad after almost hitting
            John. The look of terror on his face replayed in his head, further
            confirming that he didn&apos;t get to cry and be the sad one
            here.&nbsp;&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            Maybe it was a good thing he had lost his ability to speak, for he
            could make no excuses, he could not explain himself even if he
            wanted to. What he did was unforgivable, and he deserved whatever
            John chose as punishment. Yes, he deserved punishment, not to be
            treated with such kindness and patience he didn&apos;t even earn. He
            tried to curl into himself more, but his own body got in the way.
            Time passed like a kidney stone, every moment brought more pain, and
            time just lengthened, seemingly endless. The only telling of the
            passing of time was the sound of his heartbeat that drowned out all
            other sounds. But then what was it, a second, half, or a third of a
            second?
          </p>
          <p>
            It was an hour later that John returned. While Jayce had not moved
            physically from where he stood, his face looked haggard.
            Jayce&apos;s head was turned towards the door, but his gaze was
            fixed firmly on the ground. John approached Jayce slowly, and knelt
            beside him. John&apos;s shoulders were tense. While the look on his
            face had calmed down, his face remained expressionless.
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;Rowdy,&quot; Jayce said in a muffled voice. But no, that
            wasn&apos;t the right word.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>&quot;Ruddy,&quot; he tried again.&nbsp;</p>
          <p>
            &quot;Roddy,&quot; and again,&quot;Roy,&quot; he had to get it
            right, &quot;Rory, Sow, Worry.&quot;
          </p>
          <p>
            Did he get it right? He wasn&apos;t sure, he didn&apos;t even know
            if he&apos;d be able to hear it right if he did say it. There was a
            shift in John&apos;s breathing, so he paused.
          </p>
          <p>
            John&apos;s expression softened. The word Jayce was trying so hard
            to say became clear. John had expected to be the one having to
            apologise for disappearing, as he panicked and ran away to calm
            down. He&apos;d ran away from the emotions he felt, thinking they
            would never be accepted. But Jayce had never let him run
            before.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            A lump formed in John&rsquo;s throat. He reached out, placing his
            hands over Jayce&rsquo;s, over the fists clenched so tightly that
            his nails had dug deep into his own flesh. Gently, John pried his
            hands open. Red crescent-shaped marks lined Jayce&rsquo;s right arm,
            proof of how hard he had been holding everything in. The sight made
            the lump in his throat grow spikes. Jayce still refused to look at
            him. John hesitated, then cupped Jayce&rsquo;s chin, tilting his
            head up. Their eyes met. It was like looking at a ghost. Jayce
            stared at him expectantly...waiting. He looked just like one of
            those terrified, shaking little puppies in an animal rescue
            video.... waiting for the hit, waiting for punishment for a crime
            they didn&apos;t commit. John knew that look. He knew it too well,
            and he hated it.
          </p>
          <p>
            On his walk back, he dreaded having to soothe Jayce. He knew the
            motions of reassuring someone who&apos;d hurt him, just to make them
            feel better so as to maintain the peace. He expected to have to do
            that.&nbsp;&nbsp;He&apos;d braced himself for it. But then,
            Jayce&apos;s eyes searched John&apos;s face for any indication of
            hurt. Jayce&apos;s concern was for John first and foremost. His gaze
            expected no reassurance, and no comfort from him. Sadness weighed on
            him. He had left Jayce hanging, thinking the worst, without a
            word... whether or not he could understand it. For once, he wanted
            to use words to explain, even if they feel on deaf ears.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            He stood up, picked up the book from where it had fallen, and
            brushed it off. It had landed open, pages splayed. He closed it
            carefully, like it was something fragile, and placed it back in
            Jayce&rsquo;s hands. Something to grip onto other than his arms.
            Then, without a word, he gathered the other comics and stacked them
            into a neat pile, a bit further away.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;Fahrenheit ready,&quot; he said softly, settling beside Jayce.
          </p>
          <p>
            He petted the scar free side of his head slowly. In an unexpected
            way, despite doing it to soothe Jayce, he found himself calming down
            too with every stroke. He had forgiven Jayce the second he walked in
            through that door. John spoke, slow and steady words. While the
            words lost their meaning, the reassurance they meant to offer
            floated weightlessly between them. And despite loosing the grasp of
            their shape, in Jayce&apos;s ears they formed the sweetest lullaby.
          </p>
          <Separator>{"\u273C"}</Separator>
          <p>
            After the book incident, John started to talk to Jayce more. He
            didn&apos;t expect Jayce to understand, and for once since the
            stroke, Jayce didn&apos;t force himself to do so either. To a great
            extent, he didn&apos;t feel the need to anymore, he just let the
            words wash over him. From that point on, his progress understanding
            oddly became a lot faster. Soon, he was able to read the comic
            books, and he could understand the shorter sentences spoken by John.
            And, in just another week, he was able to listen to a full half an
            hour of an audiobook, without rest, and probably without getting
            anything wrong. Speaking still escaped him, but the progress was
            undeniable. He didn&apos;t know if it was just because his brain had
            had the time to heal, or he finally released that pressure to
            perform that was holding him back. Regardless, it was working.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            It was slow, but he allowed it to be. At first, it felt like going
            back to his childhood with dial-up internet, where images would load
            painfully slow, row by row. But over time, the gaps between each row
            got shorter. Conversations still lagged, but now, he was catching up
            faster. He wasn&rsquo;t fully there yet, but he could see the
            progress. He had to set aside the memory of the proverbial high
            speed internet, and focus on how he felt when he first connected to
            the internet. Even when slow, he hadn&apos;t known better. And now
            he was indeed starting from the beginning.
          </p>
          <Separator>{"\u273C"}</Separator>
          <p>
            As more and more of what he read became clear to him, Jayce felt
            like he didn&apos;t depend as much on John. He&apos;d tried to
            explain to John that he wanted to be independent through a
            combination of miming and drawing. Yet every time, he did not seem
            to get John to understand. But, he could read a book now. He could
            go on websites and select all the things he needed. Correctly. He
            had bought salt the other day. And it was actually salt, not sugar.
            He was sure he could manage. Yet, John kept holding him back. He
            didn&apos;t understand where he was failing to explain. And John was
            not giving him any hints. He&apos;d just say &quot;
            <em>that doesn&apos;t make any sense to me</em>&quot; and change the
            topic.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            One day, when John was at work, Jayce packed his bags and took them
            all on the very long 10 minute journey to his apartment. He
            didn&apos;t know what he expected when he opened the door, but it
            wasn&apos;t this. The apartment was spotless, just as he&apos;d left
            it. No, actually it was a bit cleaner. His one plant, a succulent
            that he had been guilt tripped into accepting sat unperturbed on his
            work desk. Those things were resilient, but he suspected John had
            come over to water it on occasion. He placed down his bags in the
            entryway, and stared into the apartment. It was his place, but it
            didn&apos;t feel like his home anymore. Had he been away too long?
            With a deep sigh, Jayce took his collection of manhwas to his
            bookcase. It was filled with non fiction titles from all the areas
            he&apos;d worked in: business, design, leadership, management,... If
            he had a question, he could usually find the answer in a book
            somewhere in his library. Though it was not quite a library, for it
            takes a thousand books to make one. He started to reorganise his
            shelves to make space for and area dedicated to the manhwas. When it
            came to fiction, he&apos;d read it digitally, or read it then donate
            it to a charity shop. Unlike the non-fiction which he kept as
            reference, he didn&apos;t like to keep fiction books. However these
            manhwas were important to him now, so they deserved to stay.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            He had to make a couple of trips to bring everything back and
            unpack. After a job well done, he picked out a book off his shelves
            and relaxed into his recliner. It was stiff and felt unnatural, but
            soon he assumed his regular reading position. While he was still
            unable to speak, it felt good to dive into a book. It felt strangely
            productive, even though he knew he had no use for what he was
            reading. Maybe he would turn to academia, where all he would do is
            research for the sake of research.
          </p>
          <p>
            That evening, John burst through his front door, sweat running
            through his hair and down his face. He was breathing heavily, as if
            he&apos;d been running. He had been running. Jayce just waved at him
            with a smile, from the armchair, somewhat bemused at the sight.
          </p>
          <p>&quot;Is this funny to you?&quot; John asked.</p>
          <p>
            Jayce nodded, once. The classic &apos;<em>of course&apos;</em>
            &nbsp;nod.
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;Do you know how panicked I was when I got home and you
            weren&apos;t there? And then, I realised all your stuff was
            missing...&quot;
          </p>
          <p>
            Jayce had predicted needing to explain to John his departure, but he
            didn&apos;t quite expect the strength of his response. He went to
            the table where a comic book with a few red bookmarks peeking out
            lied closed. He opened it, and bookmark by bookmark, he showed it to
            John.
          </p>
          <p>&quot;I am safe&quot;</p>
          <p>&quot;You don&apos;t need to worry about me.&quot;</p>
          <p>&quot;I can do this.&quot;</p>
          <p>&quot;I had to do this.&quot;</p>
          <p>&quot;I don&apos;t want to be a burden.&quot;</p>
          <p>&quot;You&apos;re not a burden...&quot; John interjected.</p>
          <p>
            Jayce stopped him with a raised hand then continued to flip through
            the book.
          </p>
          <p>&quot;I want to be useful.&quot;</p>
          <p>&quot;It&apos;s better this way.&quot;</p>
          <p>
            John waited to see if there were more pages coming, but there
            weren&apos;t. As if seeking permission to speak, he opened his mouth
            and closed it again. Jayce waved him on.
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;It&apos;s better for whom? What about me? I understand you may
            need to feel more independent, but can&apos;t you do that without
            leaving me? If you need to feel useful, I can add you to work
            meetings to listen. You can do the design work on a new game, if you
            want to do more. But, please, why does this need to mean you
            leaving?&quot;, John&apos;s voice cracked on the last question.
          </p>
          <p>
            Jayce never expected this. He looked at John without words, not that
            he could find them. There was reassurance he wanted to give, but
            more than anything, he had a lot of questions. He didn&apos;t
            realise John felt that way. While John had only started to talk
            recently, he still didn&apos;t express much. He flipped through the
            book for a good speech bubble. He went to the bookcase, picked out
            another one, and kept searching until he found one.
          </p>
          <p>&quot;I didn&apos;t know&quot;</p>
          <p>
            It was the closest he could find to a response. It didn&apos;t
            explain anything. It wasn&apos;t an excuse. It was just a statement.
            And he didn&apos;t know now what to do with it either.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>&quot;Please come back home...&quot; John pleaded.&nbsp;</p>
          <p>
            It wasn&apos;t that he didn&apos;t want to, he did. And the
            proposals John made, around work were something that he wanted. In
            fact, just as he&apos;d mentioned making a game, an idea popped into
            his head, one he would love to draw up story boards and designs for.
            It had been a while since he was able to think of anything outside
            of just... survival.
          </p>
          <p>
            No book was going to help him explain what he needed to make it
            happen. Because he wasn&apos;t entirely what the answer was either.
            All he could grasp were feelings. One thing was sure, the sofa had
            to go. The few nights they had slept together in bed, he didn&apos;t
            mind. No, that was a bald faced lie. He actually liked it, a lot.
            But John... he never said anything. One way or another.
          </p>
          <p>He lifted a finger.&nbsp;</p>
          <p>&quot;One... you have one condition?&quot; John guessed.</p>
          <p>
            Jayce nodded. He pointed at John. He made the universal sign for
            sleep of putting two praying hands on his cheek and closing his
            eyes. Then pointed at the bed in his bedroom.
          </p>
          <p>&quot;You want me to sleep in bed with you?&quot; John asked.</p>
          <p>
            Jayce was fairly sure he didn&apos;t add the &apos;
            <em>with you&apos;</em>&nbsp;in his mime act. He would have happily
            volunteered to sleep on the sofa instead. He was familiar with that
            sofa. His mind wondered about the meaning of it, but then, knowing
            John, he wasn&apos;t the type of host who makes his ailing guest
            sleep on the sofa. That must have been John&apos;s train of thought.
            He nodded, and waited for John&apos;s response.
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;Done,&quot; he hurriedly responded. He then took Jayce&apos;s
            hand and tugged it gently. &quot;Now can we please go home?&quot;
          </p>
          <p>Jayce took the succulent and followed John quietly.&nbsp;</p>
          <Separator>{"\u273C"}</Separator>
          <p>
            John kept his promises. He added Jayce on all team meetings as an
            observer, informing the team that Jayce may be listening in on some
            meetings, but will be unable to participate. It was enough for Jayce
            to still feel like he was not completely out of the loop. It gave
            him hope that, one day, he would be able to return.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            In his time, he started drawing up the designs for a game. The main
            character advances through multiple levels in search for language.
            They had lost their language, and each level is increasingly more
            difficult, just like his journey regaining his language was. With
            everything being pictures, Jayce just freely drew his ideas on
            paper. There was a meeting with the team to get their feedback on
            the designs. And they loved it. Jayce finally felt like he was
            something more again. It was a small scale game, but everyone was
            excited to make it. Their words of encouragement reminded Jayce why
            he started the company in the first place. At some point, he had
            gotten lost in impressing all of the investors and partners.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            Progress with speech was slow, excruciatingly slow. But he had the
            game to give him hope and purpose. He drew every design himself, and
            John assigned one developer to do the implementations one day a
            week. It was a slow project, but it didn&apos;t need to be fast, it
            needed to keep him motivated.
          </p>
          <p>
            At home, John would insist on Jayce reading him to sleep. He knew it
            was an exercise to help him improve, and he wanted to get it right,
            but the way John proposed it felt less like work. Somehow it reduced
            the pressure to get it right. John even tried to get him to sing to
            sleep once, claiming that studies found lyrics and songs to be more
            helpful in aphasia recovery. But in the famous words of Meatloaf,
            &quot;
            <em>I would do anything for ____, but I won&apos;t do that</em>
            &quot;.
          </p>
          <Separator>{"\u273C"}</Separator>
          <p>
            It took over a year for Jayce to be able to speak again. And while
            he found a voice, it didn&apos;t feel quite the same as it used to
            be. John designed a return to work plan for Jayce. It revolved
            around doing his usual duties, but restricting them in scope to that
            one game he had developed over his medical leave. At first, John
            would sit in on meetings, in case he was needed. Jayce would look at
            him for reassurance that things were coming out right. Despite the
            progress he had made, the fear of relapse remained forever present.
            The fear that people will not understand him, that he is completely
            speaking nonsense, stayed with him throughout. Speaking in spite of
            the fear at every step was exhausting.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            John remained his safety net in meetings: someone whom he could turn
            to, there at all times. But after some time, John told him he was
            going to stop coming. Jayce&apos;s shoulders slumped. He understood,
            he had inconvenienced him enough.
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;Just to be clear, I am not leaving because the meetings are a
            waste of my time. I am leaving, because for the last month I have
            been doing my work during those meetings and you didn&apos;t need me
            once. I can keep working from your meeting room, but I frankly
            don&apos;t see the point,&quot; John made sure to explain.
          </p>
          <p>
            Jayce would notice his grammar slipping sometimes. But he&apos;d
            look around and no-one would seem to care. In fact, he wasn&apos;t
            even sure they noticed. Slowly, things were turning back to how they
            used to be. Other than the success of the game that was inspired by
            his aphasia, everyone seemed to have forgotten about the whole
            incident.
          </p>
          <Separator>{"\u273C"}</Separator>
          <p>
            The one thing that did not return to how it used to be was
            Jayce&apos;s relationship with John. While John was still mostly
            quiet, he now made sure to say the important things. Making sure not
            to leave Jayce guessing. And Jayce learned, at first out of
            necessity, how to say a lot more while saying less. John stopped
            running away, and Jayce found himself able to give John space when
            he needed it. However, as their work lives returned to normal, Jayce
            kept wondering what would happen to their private lives. The state
            of their current relationship was confusing. It wasn&apos;t
            unpleasant, but there were too many questions left unanswered. For
            Jayce, in spite of all the routine, the relationship felt unstable.
            So, that evening as they were settling in on the living room sofa
            for an evening film, he said that phrase everyone dreads to hear in
            a relationship.
          </p>
          <p>&quot;John, we need to talk.&quot;</p>
          <p>&quot;Good or bad?&quot; he asked.</p>
          <p>&quot;I don&apos;t know, yet.&quot;</p>
          <p>
            There was a furrow in his brow as he said that. John put away the
            remote and looked straight at Jayce.
          </p>
          <p>&quot;Ok...&quot; he said as an invitation to say more.</p>
          <p>&quot;I have question.&quot;</p>
          <p>
            The statement was meant with silence. Not the silence of
            indifference, but that which gave him space to find the words. One
            leftover tell from his aphasia was that Jayce&apos;s grammar dropped
            when he was nervous. So, John gave him space.
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;Why didn&apos;t you speak before?&quot; Jayce finally asked.
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;I always thought speaking makes things worse. Like, whatever I
            said, eventually, I would say the wrong thing, and that would make
            my life harder. Staying silent always seemed like the better
            choice.&quot;
          </p>
          <p>&quot;Even with me?&quot;</p>
          <p>
            &quot;Sometimes. I thought that we became friends while I
            didn&apos;t speak, so I didn&apos;t need to afterwards either. But
            if I am honest with myself, there was a part of me that, whenever
            the temptation to say something came up, feared that it was too
            late, and it would be the wrong thing to say. Anyway, it was never
            anything interesting...&quot;
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;Did you think all that you said had to be interesting?&quot;
          </p>
          <p>&quot;Yes?&quot;</p>
          <p>
            &quot;Do you think all that I was blabbing on about was
            interesting?&quot;
          </p>
          <p>&quot;Yes,&quot; he said without missing a beat.</p>
          <p>
            &nbsp;Jayce was a bit taken aback by the response. There was no hint
            of sarcasm in John&apos;s voice or face. A warm feeling enveloped
            him.
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;I always found it interesting to listen to you. Also, you had
            this way of making yourself understood that I really admired.
            Somehow, you&apos;d manage to communicate so well and never be
            misunderstood,&quot; John explained.
          </p>
          <p>Jayce stared at John in disbelief.</p>
          <p>&quot;You think I have never been misunderstood?&quot;</p>
          <p>&quot;Yes?&quot;</p>
          <p>Jayce had to hold back a laugh.&nbsp;</p>
          <p>
            &quot;I have been misunderstood so many times. You misunderstood me
            quite a few times that I can think of.&quot;
          </p>
          <p>&quot;I have? I am so sorry!&quot;</p>
          <p>
            &quot;What for? Sometimes I needed to explain things a little more,
            but I guess you wouldn&apos;t see that as a misunderstanding... It
            was easy, and you&apos;d always listen. But, at times, you
            misunderstood me in much better, smarter or nobler ways that I ever
            intended. A lot of times, actually. In the end, I let you
            misunderstand me, because it was better that way.&quot;
          </p>
          <p>&quot;So... you don&apos;t fear being misunderstood.&quot;</p>
          <p>
            &quot;Absolutely not. I am terrified of it. Why do you think I talk
            so much? But with you... it wasn&apos;t half bad. Even when I was an
            absolute prick, and said things that should have ruined our
            friendship...&quot;
          </p>
          <p>&quot;You have never...&quot; John interjected.</p>
          <p>
            &quot;Oh, I have. You somehow still managed to see the best in me.
            At some point, I just wanted to live up to that image you&apos;ve
            built of me in your head, because it was kinder that my own.&quot;
          </p>
          <p>&quot;I can&apos;t think of any.&quot;</p>
          <p>
            &quot;Just because you can&apos;t think of any, doesn&apos;t mean
            they don&apos;t exist. Just the sheer number of times when I messed
            up, and you covered for me, even if I blamed you, unfairly.&quot;
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;But, you always took accountability and fixed it, and
            apologised afterwards.&quot;
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;I did, but that shouldn&apos;t excuse how I&apos;ve treated
            you at times. You also got me out of some nasty situations. And
            every time you chose to forgive me, even if you didn&apos;t have
            to.&quot;
          </p>
          <p>&quot;But, that&apos;s normal...&quot;</p>
          <p>
            &quot;Yet, you don&apos;t think you would get the same
            treatment?&quot;
          </p>
          <p>
            John had nothing to say to that. The true answer to the question was
            the same as the incorrect answer.
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;Still, that was never a risk to ruin our friendship, it was
            just work.&quot;
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;Work can ruin friendships. Regardless, I&apos;ve don&apos;t
            worse.&quot;
          </p>
          <p>John stared blankly at Jayce.</p>
          <p>
            &quot;And behold, how the misunderstanding persists, thus protecting
            our friendship.&quot;
          </p>
          <p>
            The admission itched on his tongue. He benefitted from the
            misunderstanding, yet it never sat too comfortably with him. Since
            they&apos;d been living together, sleeping in the same bed, however,
            the guilt kept building, as things could no longer be left as they
            were. John stared at Jayce, curiosity burning in his eyes. It was
            now or never. Jayce sighed.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;I don&apos;t think we can continue living together, if you
            don&apos;t know. And I am not sure if we&apos;ll be able to continue
            living together once you know...&quot; he explained, stalling for
            time. &quot;Remember that time I kissed you, at uni?&quot;
          </p>
          <p>&quot;Yeah, you were drunk.&quot;</p>
          <p>
            &quot;I wasn&apos;t drunk. Though I admit, I never corrected that
            misunderstanding. However, even if I was, still not an excuse.&quot;
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;You&apos;re just more touchy feely and flirtatious like
            that... it&apos;s just jokes... plus, you never did it
            again...&quot;
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;John, a joke needs to be funny. Kissing you, without consent,
            isn&apos;t funny, it&apos;s assault. But, I let you misunderstand me
            because it saved our friendship. I am not touchy feely or
            flirtatious with everyone.&quot;
          </p>
          <p>&quot;What do you mean?&quot;</p>
          <p>
            Jayce, self-anointed king of words, before stroke, struggled to
            allow the words out. His confession was right there, in the right
            moment, just waiting to said, yet he struggled.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;You are the only person I ever said &apos;<em>I love you</em>
            &apos; to. And I meant it every time,&quot; Jayce took a deep
            breath. The hard part was over. &quot;More than just a friend. But
            I&apos;ll happily continue to love you as a friend, because you are
            the most important person in my life, and I&apos;d be a damn fool to
            lose that. However, our arrangement here... it would be unethical
            for me to allow it to continue without telling you this. I have
            alreadt let it go on for too long...&quot;&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            Jayce searched John&apos;s face for any response. He could tell he
            was thinking, but with every second he grew more scared. He stood up
            to leave.
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;If you&apos;re uncomfortable knowing this... if you hate me
            for hiding it for so long... I understand... I can leave... I will
            give you space, I just hope I haven&apos;t ruined... us.&quot;
          </p>
          <p>
            Jayce wanted to run away and hide, but he had to be calm. He walked
            towards the door, slowly, only for a hand to grab his wrist. The
            grip was shaky and weak, which made it even harder to break. Jayce
            froze, not turning around to face John. He was too scared.
          </p>
          <p>&quot;I don&apos;t hate you, I could never hate you...&quot;</p>
          <p>
            The reassurance quelled his guilt. The confession made it undeniable
            that his relationship with John was always too much for what it was,
            but too little for what he wanted it to be. He had kept himself
            stuck in this relationship limbo for many years. Not being hated may
            not be enough anymore. Over the past year of living together, the
            boundaries that kept his life and relationships somewhat sane had
            blurred into a mess of uncertainty. He had depended on John for a
            great deal of that time, but now that he had regained his
            independence, he had to either step away or clarify the
            relationship. The more he thought about it, the more he wanted to
            run away, yet he couldn&apos;t bring himself to just break away.
            Then a gentle tug came. He turned around to face John, who had stood
            up. When their eyes met, John hesitated for a moment, then gently,
            he placed a kiss on Jayce&apos;s lips. It was less of a kiss and
            more of a peck, a gentle touching of the lips. When the initial
            surprise dissipated, Jayce finally asked.
          </p>
          <p>&quot;What does this mean?&quot;</p>
          <p>&quot;I thought I had shown you how I felt.&quot;</p>
          <p>
            &quot;Sometimes, no amount of showing can make up for the words not
            spoken.&quot;
          </p>
          <p>
            Jayce needed to hear it. He couldn&apos;t live in the maybes and
            what ifs. John exhaled. He hadn&apos;t realised he had been holding
            his breath all that time.&nbsp;
          </p>
          <p>
            &quot;I love you,&quot; he said. Once he said those three words,
            they felt like the easiest words he had ever spoken.
          </p>
          <p>
            For once, Jayce no longer needed words or elaborate speeches. He
            pulled John in for a hungry kiss, one that would make up for all the
            time they had lost in the words not said and the things too freely
            spoken.
          </p>

          <h2>
            If you liked this story consider purchasing it{" "}
